the virtual reality realm has progressed  from a hypothetical sci-fi fantasy to a  competitive marketable reality really  quickly in fact in the last few weeks a  bunch of new companies have joined the  VR race so I'm here to give you the gist  on some of the biggest offerings first  up let's talk about the oculus rift the  oculus rift burst onto the scene via  kickstarter in 2012 we left it up  because it was essentially the first of  its caliber in VR and it was coming from  an independent developer you know before  Facebook bought it the proposed consumer  version which is currently in  development will have integrated audio  and better head and positional tracking  and the presently available developer  kit models on top of that the device  will feature a 1080p low persistence of  vision o LED display as well as 7-inch  stereoscopic displays and 24-bit color  depth the most commonly mentioned issues  the rift is combating are the weight of  the device as well as ensuring  affordability and improved performance  then there's project Morpheus which is  your playstation 4 option this one is  going to launch early next year and  features nine tracking LEDs which work  to make positional tracking as accurate  as possible it has a 5.7 inch screen and  support a 100 degree field of view the  latest model features a 1920 x 1080 oh  LED display and a massive 120 Hertz  refresh rate sony have also touted their  strapless sleek design and emphasis on  comfort they've shifted most of the way  to the top of the head distancing  themselves from the more uncomfortable  traditional goggle style headset  basically this is the one you'll be  picking up for your ps4 vr needs next up  we've got razor whose VR headset is  backed by Ubisoft and will only run you  200 bucks the first of the lot to drop a  price razors offering is open source and  has virtual reality goggles facturers  centex joining razor at the hell  as with all things VR there is a dev kit  and a consumer kit the first of which is  now available from raises website the  headset comes with a full HD oh LED  display which offers higher contrast  with low latency thanks to foster pixel  switching they also reckon it's going to  be comfy which is always a plus with  these things not to be left out AMD have  also plunged into virtual reality with  their entry called liquid VR liquid VR  is using AMD's GPU software and hardware  systems in the hopes to reduce latency  to less than 10 milliseconds ideally  that'll keep you immersed throughout the  experience and should also stop you  getting motion sick which happens when  the environment feels too artificial and  your brain can't adapt quickly enough  liquid via also hopes to work with other  VR devices that are on the market they  also want to have a plug-and-play sort  of system with their AMD Radeon graphics  cards to keep the experience seamless  although there's a lot going on in the  virtual reality space right now Alvin  HTC vive headset seems to be getting  most of the attention and for good  reason the developer edition uses to  1200 x 1080 displays which refresh at 90  frames per second this promises to  eliminate the jitter that can plague VR  devices and unlike other offerings with  vive you can physically walk around a  virtual environment the headset which is  complete with gyro sensor accelerometer  and laser position sensor is coupled  with steam VR base stations which track  your physical location in spaces up to  15 square feet val have also introduced  a room scale tracking system called  lighthouse which will enable high speed  pinpoint tracking for any number of  targets although they plan to make this  freely available to hardware  manufacturers vive is also partnered  with HBO Lionsgate and Google and with a  name like valve behind it big things are  expected from the headset particularly  given that valve is shooting for  seamless integration with steam VR  titles which are growing by the day and  currently include games like tf2 Proteus  and portal currently the main concern  will be how val  an HTC will keep the price down with all  these technologies and peripherals not  to mention the requirement of a really  beefy PC to run the whole thing at it's  optimized resolution let me know down in  the comments which VR headset you reckon  is the one to watch I'll catch again  next week
 

